-## 4. Lemma (covering radius on an intersection of disks)
-
-Let \(K=\bigcap_j\overline{\mathbb{D}}(c_j,r_j)\) be a nonempty intersection of closed disks (convex, compact). For a finite set of sites \(\mathcal{S}\), write \(d_{\mathcal{S}}(p)=\min_{s\in\mathcal{S}}\|p-s\|\) and \(\rho(\mathcal{S},K)=\max_{p\in K}d_{\mathcal{S}}(p)\) (and \(+\infty\) if \(\mathcal{S}=\emptyset\)).
-
-**Lemma 3.** \(\rho(\mathcal{S},K)\) is attained at a point of one of the following classes:
-
-1. A Voronoi vertex of \(\mathcal{S}\) in \(K\) (circumcentre of three sites, empty circumcircle).
-2. An intersection of a Voronoi edge (perpendicular bisector of two sites) with a circle \(\partial\overline{\mathbb{D}}(c_j,r_j)\), lying in \(K\).
-3. A radial far extremum of a site \(s\) on a circle \(\partial\overline{\mathbb{D}}(c_j,r_j)\): the point of that circle collinear with \(s\) and \(c_j\) and farthest from \(s\), if it lies in \(K\).
-4. A vertex of \(K\) (intersection of two bounding circles).
-
-Proof. \(d_{\mathcal{S}}\) is the pointwise minimum of convex functions. In the interior of a Voronoi cell, \(d_{\mathcal{S}}(p)=\|p-s\|\) has no critical point except \(p=s\) (a minimum). Interior maxima can occur only at Voronoi vertices. On \(\partial K\), each connected piece of \(\mathrm{cell}(s)\cap\partial K\) is a circular arc; \(\|p-s\|\) on a circle is stationary only on the line \(s\,c_j\), and the far intersection is the only candidate for a maximum. Endpoints of those arcs are Voronoi-edge ∩ \(\partial K\) or vertices of \(K\).
-
-Therefore \(\overline{\mathbb{D}}(Q,1000)\cap\mathrm{Res}_{\mathrm{cont}}(\mathcal{S})=\emptyset\) iff \(\rho(\mathcal{S},K_Q)\le 1000\). The code’s `covering_radius_on_disks` enumerates (1)–(4) and returns \(\max d_{\mathcal{S}}\) on that candidate set (plus extra boundary samples, which can only raise the estimate). Skip uses a proven upper bound \(U\le 1000\) only. Expanding \(K\) then comparing to \(1000+\varepsilon\) does **not** imply \(\rho(K)\le 1000\). If the numeric estimate is inconclusive, keep the scout.
-
-Empty \(\mathcal{S}\): \(\rho=+\infty\), every scout is kept. A site that covers its own disk: \(\rho(\{Q\},K_Q)=1000\), the scout is dropped after it has been scanned.
-
-For this policy \(K_Q\) is always a two-disk lens \(\overline{\mathbb{D}}(Q,1000)\cap\mathbb{A}\) (or \(\mathbb{A}\) itself when testing the whole arena). In the plane, an interior Voronoi vertex needs three sites; a two-site diagram contributes only a bisector, whose maximum on \(K\) sits on \(\partial K\). One site has its maximum at a far radial or a vertex of \(K\). So classes (1)–(4) are exhaustive for this \(K\). Extra 72-angle boundary samples are a skip-veto floor, not the completeness argument. A polar-grid hunt (24 random site sets, 36×72 samples on \(\mathbb{A}\) and on \(K_{R_0}\)) never found a grid point with \(d_{\mathcal{S}}\) more than \(8\,\mathrm{cm}\) above the enumerated \(\rho\); that hunt is not the theorem.
