-## 5. The implemented policy
-
-Silent-channel discovery does the following, and only the following, to the 9-net.
-
-1. **Scan obligation.** While silent channels remain, \(n_{\mathrm{found}}<16\), and \(\mathrm{Res}_{\mathrm{cont}}\ne\emptyset\), `_covering_goals` returns a waypoint for every remaining scout with \(\rho(\mathcal{S},K_Q)>1000\). `_certificate_fallback_scouts` restores any unused net point that still meets residual (Lemma 2a).
-2. **Waypoint.** `_covering_waypoint(Q)` may return a cheaper pose \(p\) (current pose, a point of here\(\to Q\), a leftover witness ray, a 1-bearing LOB sample, a 2-bearing SEC). That \(p\) is used only if \(\rho(\mathcal{S}\cup\{p\},K_Q)\le 1000\), i.e. \(K_Q\subseteq\bigcup\mathrm{disk}(\mathcal{S}\cup\{p\},1000)\). The scout centre always satisfies this because \(K_Q\subseteq\overline{\mathbb{D}}(Q,1000)\). So replacing \(Q\) by \(p\) preserves covering of that cell.
-3. **Silent scan.** `_batch_scan_scout(p)` measures every remaining silent channel at \(p\) whenever \(\overline{\mathbb{D}}(p,1000)\) meets \(\mathrm{Res}_{\mathrm{cont}}\) (true for a covering waypoint of a live cell). The pose is marked a discovery station only if those silent measurements happened. Finite lists are not used to empty `silent`.
-4. **Prune.** After a scan, drop \(Q\) iff \(\rho(\mathcal{S},K_Q)\le 1000\). Do not drop \(Q\) because the robot is near \(Q\), because a 30 m neighbour was visited, or because a finite polar grid is covered.
-5. **Stop.** No silent channels, or \(n_{\mathrm{found}}\ge 16\), or \(\mathrm{Res}_{\mathrm{cont}}=\emptyset\).
-
-Time-layer choices (TSP order, D-opt, savings-rule inserts, path-as-sensor nibbles, LOB homing) may reorder visits or add extra discovery disks. They do not delete a 9-net cell whose continuum leftover is nonempty. A D-opt city may replace a covering city in the current TSP only when that D-opt pose continuum-covers the same cell; if the D-opt visit does not scan silent, the cell remains in `remaining_scouts` for the next iteration.
